# React 19

## Rules of React
- Render is idempotent and pure: no mutation of props, state, or context during render. Side effects belong in event handlers or `useEffect`, never the render body.
- Hooks are called at the top level only — never in conditions, loops, callbacks, or after an early return. Never call a component as a function (`Foo()`); render `<Foo />`.

## State and effects
- One source of truth per piece of state; if two pieces can disagree, derive one from the other. Keep state in the component that uses it until a second consumer appears, then lift exactly one level. Prop drilling stops at 2 levels.
- Server state lives in the react-query cache — never mirrored into `useState` or a store, never fetched in an Effect.
- No Effect for derived state. Effects are for external systems, intervals, and imperative third-party sync; the cleanup undoes whatever the Effect set up. Every async Effect guards against out-of-order resolution (`ignore` flag or `AbortController`). Never disable StrictMode to hide a broken cleanup.
- An Effect never writes state its own dependency array reads. A value it reads but must not re-run on lives in a ref. Never trim the dep array to silence the lint rule.
- A component is controlled or uncontrolled for its whole life. Reset state with a `key` prop, not an Effect that syncs state to props.
- Keys are stable, unique ids on the outermost element the `map` returns — never the array index for a list that can reorder, grow, or shrink.
- Never read or write `ref.current` during render. A hook that requires a context throws when used outside its Provider.

## Memoization (React Compiler)
- No `React.memo`, `useMemo`, or `useCallback` preemptively. Only for an Effect dependency that would otherwise retrigger every render, a callback handed to non-compiled code that stores the reference, or a profiled hotspot. Do not bulk-remove existing memoization either.

## Boundaries and splitting
- Every route element and every feature's public entry component renders inside `ErrorBoundary` (`@/shared/ui/error-boundary`) with `resetKeys`. Hooks live in the child the boundary wraps, never above it. Async and event-handler errors use `try/catch` plus `notify.error()`.
- `lazy()` every route. Inline JSX blocks over ~20 lines become a nested component in `{component}/ui/{child}/` (`rules/component-structure.mdc`), not a second component in the same file.

## JSX
- More than one prop: one prop per line, closing `/>` or `>` on its own line. Element content starts on its own line, not inline with the tags.
- JSX references handlers; it never defines them. Every `on*` prop receives a named function declared before `return` (`handleOpenChange`) — no arrow or function expression inside an `on*` prop, not even a one-liner. A render prop may stay inline.
- No nested ternary, in JSX or in the values it renders. No ternary inside JSX: compute the value before `return`, early-return a whole-branch swap, `&&` with a boolean for show/hide, or a lookup map keyed by a union. The left side of `&&` is a boolean, never a number that can render `0`.
- A closed-set prop (`variant`, `size`, `type`, `side`, `align`, `orientation`) takes the constant the primitive exports from its `constants.ts` (`ButtonVariant.Outline`, `ButtonType.Submit`) — never a string literal. Omit a prop equal to the default: `Button` defaults `type` to `ButtonType.Button` and `variant` to `ButtonVariant.Default`.

